1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,666
Logistic regression and LDA both turn features into the probability of a class.

2
00:00:05,666 --> 00:00:09,064
Logistic regression models that probability directly.

3
00:00:09,064 --> 00:00:12,334
LDA models how the features look within each class,

4
00:00:12,334 --> 00:00:14,771
then flips it around with Bayes' rule.

5
00:00:14,771 --> 00:00:19,003
Let's do both, using the YourCabs model from practice midterm one.

6
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:25,844
Logistic regression passes a linear score, z, through the logistic function.

7
00:00:25,844 --> 00:00:29,155
The output is the probability that Y equals one.

8
00:00:29,155 --> 00:00:32,743
When z is zero, the probability is exactly one half.

9
00:00:32,743 --> 00:00:36,331
As z grows, it approaches one, but never reaches it.

10
00:00:37,087 --> 00:00:40,618
That gives four true or false facts the exams love.

11
00:00:40,618 --> 00:00:43,596
Y itself is zero or one, not a probability.

12
00:00:43,596 --> 00:00:48,098
Predicted probabilities are always strictly between zero and one.

13
00:00:48,098 --> 00:00:53,568
Logistic regression makes no assumption about how X is distributed; that's LDA.

14
00:00:53,568 --> 00:00:56,823
And the formula on screen is exactly the model.

15
00:00:58,433 --> 00:01:02,026
Here's the second logistic model from practice midterm one,

16
00:01:02,026 --> 00:01:05,984
predicting whether a driver cancels from how the ride was booked.

17
00:01:05,984 --> 00:01:08,419
The output reads like linear regression,

18
00:01:08,419 --> 00:01:11,098
but the estimates are on the log odds scale.

19
00:01:11,853 --> 00:01:15,878
To predict, compute z first, then convert it.

20
00:01:15,878 --> 00:01:23,301
A rider books on the mobile site, so OnlineBooking is zero and MobileSiteBooking is

21
00:01:23,301 --> 00:01:23,658
one.

22
00:01:23,658 --> 00:01:28,398
z is minus 4.6625 plus 2.3231, which is minus 2.3394.

23
00:01:28,398 --> 00:01:34,301
The probability is one over one plus e to the 2.3394, about 0.088.

24
00:01:34,301 --> 00:01:39,309
The question also says the driver's vehicle is model 65,

25
00:01:39,309 --> 00:01:44,049
but this model has no vehicle variable, so ignore it.

26
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:49,198
Sanity check: a negative z always means a probability below one half.

27
00:01:49,198 --> 00:01:54,401
And you can leave the answer as an expression if your calculator has no e to the x

28
00:01:54,401 --> 00:01:54,655
key.

29
00:01:56,267 --> 00:02:00,401
The linear score z is the log of the odds, p over one minus p.

30
00:02:00,401 --> 00:02:03,668
So each coefficient says how the log odds change.

31
00:02:03,668 --> 00:02:08,736
Exponentiate it, and you get the factor the odds are multiplied by when that

32
00:02:08,736 --> 00:02:10,136
feature rises by one.

33
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:14,763
For mobile site bookings, e to the 2.3231 is about 10.2.

34
00:02:14,763 --> 00:02:18,881
A mobile booking has about ten times the odds of cancelling,

35
00:02:18,881 --> 00:02:22,518
compared with a booking made through neither channel.

36
00:02:22,518 --> 00:02:24,782
Online bookings: about 5.4 times.

37
00:02:24,782 --> 00:02:29,929
That's the odds, not the probability, though with probabilities this small,

38
00:02:29,929 --> 00:02:31,164
the two are close.

39
00:02:32,767 --> 00:02:36,895
The first model on practice midterm one included VehicleModelId,

40
00:02:36,895 --> 00:02:42,119
a categorical variable with 14 levels, but the output shows only 13 coefficients.

41
00:02:42,119 --> 00:02:46,247
That's dummy coding: one level, here model 10, is the reference,

42
00:02:46,247 --> 00:02:48,053
absorbed into the intercept.

43
00:02:48,053 --> 00:02:52,890
A fourteenth dummy would add nothing, since all fourteen always sum to one,

44
00:02:52,890 --> 00:02:55,986
which is perfectly collinear with the intercept.

45
00:02:56,753 --> 00:02:58,540
Why remove VehicleModelId?

46
00:02:58,540 --> 00:03:03,349
The exam answer: every one of its coefficients has a p-value near one,

47
00:03:03,349 --> 00:03:04,930
so none is significant.

48
00:03:05,687 --> 00:03:09,938
To go deeper, look at the standard errors: around 7,800.

49
00:03:09,938 --> 00:03:12,444
That's a red flag for separation.

50
00:03:12,444 --> 00:03:16,772
Some vehicle models probably had no cancellations at all,

51
00:03:16,772 --> 00:03:23,150
so their coefficients run off toward infinity, and their p-values stop meaning much.

52
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:29,148
And AIC, where lower is better, actually prefers the first model: 644.88 versus

53
00:03:29,148 --> 00:03:29,679
659.63.

54
00:03:31,267 --> 00:03:32,940
LDA takes the other route.

55
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:37,831
It assumes that within each class, the features follow a multivariate normal

56
00:03:37,831 --> 00:03:41,756
distribution, with the same covariance matrix in every class.

57
00:03:41,756 --> 00:03:45,618
That's question four on practice midterm two, and it's true.

58
00:03:46,387 --> 00:03:51,462
Then it flips those class distributions into a probability with Bayes' rule: the

59
00:03:51,462 --> 00:03:54,063
prior for class k times its density at x,

60
00:03:54,063 --> 00:03:57,488
divided by the same thing summed over all the classes.

61
00:03:58,253 --> 00:04:03,394
Practice midterm two, question twelve: 90 percent of students pass.

62
00:04:03,394 --> 00:04:09,301
The probability of passing given 10 hours of study is 0.9 times f pass at 10,

63
00:04:09,301 --> 00:04:13,598
over 0.9 times f pass at 10 plus 0.1 times f fail at 10.

64
00:04:13,598 --> 00:04:14,825
That's answer B.

65
00:04:14,825 --> 00:04:20,963
The decoys drop the priors, drop the denominator, or keep the prior only on top.

66
00:04:22,567 --> 00:04:23,889
Two more LDA facts.

67
00:04:23,889 --> 00:04:27,300
Categorical features break the normal assumption,

68
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:30,641
but you can still dummy-code them and train LDA.

69
00:04:30,641 --> 00:04:36,487
So the claim that it's not possible to train LDA with categorical features is false.

70
00:04:37,253 --> 00:04:41,775
With two normal classes, equal variances, and equal priors,

71
00:04:41,775 --> 00:04:45,836
the decision boundary sits halfway between the means.

72
00:04:45,836 --> 00:04:48,902
In question eleven's setup, that's 4.75.

73
00:04:48,902 --> 00:04:51,890
The exam's 4.3 is just a chosen cutoff.

74
00:04:52,653 --> 00:04:54,170
So which should you use?

75
00:04:54,170 --> 00:04:56,951
Logistic regression assumes nothing about X,

76
00:04:56,951 --> 00:04:59,984
so it's safer when features are far from normal.

77
00:04:59,984 --> 00:05:05,039
LDA's stronger assumption pays off when it holds, especially with small samples,

78
00:05:05,039 --> 00:05:08,009
and it handles three or more classes naturally.

79
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:10,247
To recap.

80
00:05:10,247 --> 00:05:14,774
Logistic regression outputs one over one plus e to the minus z,

81
00:05:14,774 --> 00:05:19,446
always strictly between zero and one, with no assumption about X.

82
00:05:19,446 --> 00:05:24,692
To predict, compute z using only the model's own variables, then convert.

83
00:05:24,692 --> 00:05:27,135
e to the beta multiplies the odds.

84
00:05:27,135 --> 00:05:32,309
A factor with K levels becomes K minus 1 dummies, and p-values near one,

85
00:05:32,309 --> 00:05:35,112
or huge standard errors, are red flags.

86
00:05:35,112 --> 00:05:39,137
And LDA assumes normal classes with a shared covariance,

87
00:05:39,137 --> 00:05:41,867
then uses Bayes' rule with the priors.

88
00:05:41,867 --> 00:05:42,874
Next up: CART.
